# EA System secrets (Slack tokens)
# Copy to ~/.ea/.env.secrets and fill in your values.
# IMPORTANT: chmod 600 ~/.ea/.env.secrets — this file must NOT be readable
# by the ea-bot subprocess. Only the owner (your user account) should read it.
#
# If you are migrating from a single .env file, move these two lines from
# ~/.ea/.env to ~/.ea/.env.secrets and set permissions:
#   chmod 600 ~/.ea/.env.secrets
#   chown $(whoami):$(whoami) ~/.ea/.env.secrets

# Slack — get these from your Slack app at api.slack.com/apps
# Bot token: OAuth & Permissions → Bot User OAuth Token
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-your-token-here

# App-level token: Basic Information → App-Level Tokens → Generate
# Needs connections:write scope
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-your-app-level-token-here

# Zulip: ONLY when chat.frontend is "zulip" in ea-config.json (docs/SETUP.md
# Step 3e). The EA bot's API key, printed by scripts/zulip_bootstrap.py.
# The bot, cron agents, plugins and chat_notify.py read it from here.
# ZULIP_API_KEY=your-zulip-bot-api-key

# Microsoft Graph client secret — ONLY for GRAPH_AUTH_METHOD=confidential.
# Created by your Entra admin under the app registration's "Certificates &
# secrets". This is sensitive; keep it here (chmod 600), not in .env.
# In the hardened (mediated) deployment the owner-side mail mediator loads this
# file and runs graph_client for the agent, so the secret is NEVER passed to the
# ea-bot subprocess. Only in an unmediated deployment does run-agent.sh pass
# GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET through to the agent. See docs/SECURITY.md (Layer 5).
# GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-value-here
